from pathlib import Path
import json,html
m=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];src=m/'src'
posts=[
('name-downloaded-pdfs','Stop losing context when a file is called download.pdf','A descriptive name starts with the source page, not a guess about the document.', '''<p>A folder full of <code>download.pdf</code> and numbered copies makes a later handoff difficult. Before saving a large batch, identify where each proposed name comes from.</p><h2>Use the strongest available label</h2><ol><li>Check the visible link text. “2025 Annual Report” is usually more useful than a URL such as <code>d001.pdf</code>.</li><li>If the link just says “Download PDF,” check its own row or document card for a descriptive title.</li><li>Retain a year only if it appears in the source label. The download date is not necessarily the document date.</li><li>Preview several names and correct ambiguous titles before starting the whole batch.</li></ol><p>Existing free downloaders may already help: Simple Mass Downloader supports a Link text naming mask. That works well when links are descriptive; a generic link can still produce a generic filename.</p><h2>Keep a source index</h2><p>Record the source page, original URL, intended name and actual saved path. This preserves context if two documents have similar titles. It is an index, not a guarantee that a file is authentic or current.</p><p>Batch Grab’s beta suggests names from visible link text and relevant nearby labels. You can edit the names before saving. It does not read inside PDFs, infer authors or rename an existing local document library. Try a small subset on your actual page first.</p>'''),
('check-bulk-downloads','A completed download is not a checked document','Reconcile the folder with the files you intended to collect.', '''<p>A browser can successfully save the wrong response. For example, a link may return a sign-in page instead of the report you expected. A completed transfer alone does not verify its contents.</p><h2>Use a short reconciliation checklist</h2><ol><li>Write down the expected documents independently of the downloader’s result count.</li><li>Review failed or interrupted transfers separately from completed ones.</li><li>Open a sample of saved files. Check the title, format and relevant pages.</li><li>Investigate a suspiciously small file or unexpected extension. Do not assume changing its extension repairs its contents.</li><li>Retry temporary failures after checking the source. Do not bypass a login, CAPTCHA, subscription or access restriction.</li></ol><h2>Keep unresolved items visible</h2><p>A useful handoff says what is missing. Record failed URLs and error details rather than silently removing them from the list.</p><p>Batch Grab records Chrome’s completed and interrupted outcomes and provides manual retries. Its source-and-outcome CSV includes actual saved paths and failures. Runtime “completed” means Chrome saved the response; it does not mean Batch Grab verified the PDF or its contents. That distinction matters even when the download process worked correctly.</p>'''),
('client-document-handoff','Build a document folder another person can understand','Names, folders and sources belong in the same handoff.', '''<p>A client should not need your browser history to understand the folder you deliver. A simple structure is often enough: one project root, a small number of meaningful sections, descriptive names, and a source index.</p><h2>A fictional example</h2><p><code>Client research / Finance and Governance / 2025 Annual Report.pdf</code></p><p>This is illustrative data, not a customer result. Use the year and title shown in the real source. Do not add an “official” or “final” label unless you have established it.</p><h2>Before the handoff</h2><ul><li>Check that the file list matches the agreed scope.</li><li>Resolve duplicate-looking names without overwriting an existing file.</li><li>Separate unresolved failures from delivered files.</li><li>Keep original source URLs and actual local filenames together.</li><li>Remove private access tokens or local path details before sharing the index externally.</li></ul><p>Batch Grab can suggest section folders from page headings, lets you edit names and subfolders, and exports a local source/outcome CSV. It saves under Chrome’s Downloads directory. It does not share files with clients, merge multiple pages into a project or check whether the underlying document is the latest version.</p><p>Use a five- or ten-file sample with the person receiving the handoff. Their ability to identify the files matters more than an impressive resource count.</p>'''),
('resource-counts','Why two downloaders show different file counts','Compare resource URLs and relevance before comparing totals.', '''<p>Two extensions can scan the same apparent webpage and show different totals without either having found more useful documents.</p><h2>Four things to check</h2><ol><li><strong>Page state:</strong> images and links may appear after loading, interaction or scrolling.</li><li><strong>Filters:</strong> one list may include ordinary webpage links, logos and icons, while another shows only document candidates.</li><li><strong>Responsive images:</strong> a small thumbnail and a larger srcset alternative can be two URLs for the same picture.</li><li><strong>Duplicates:</strong> tools may deduplicate exact URLs or group size variants differently.</li></ol><h2>Compare the intended files</h2><p>Use the same page, login state, wait and scroll position. Compare actual unique URLs, then inspect which ones are relevant and accessible. A link named “Download” is not proof that it serves a downloadable file.</p><p>Batch Grab keeps ordinary files and links accessible in All files, while Suggested prioritizes likely content and documents. Additional uncertain image alternatives appear in Review. Those classifications are rules, not semantic understanding or a promise that every candidate will download. No tool should be expected to recover protected content simply because its displayed count is larger.</p>'''),
('popup-download-queue','Can you close the popup during a batch?','First distinguish an accepted job from an unsent selection.', '''<p>A popup closing should not be confused with a batch being submitted. Selecting files is not the same as saving download jobs.</p><h2>A small safe trial</h2><ol><li>Review a few files and their final names.</li><li>Click Download Selected and wait for the jobs-saved confirmation.</li><li>Close the popup while keeping Chrome running.</li><li>Reopen it and inspect completed, pending and failed outcomes.</li><li>Check the actual downloaded files, not just the interface.</li></ol><p>Batch Grab stores accepted jobs in its background queue. Popup closure and a service-worker restart were tested on controlled fixtures, including a 120-file batch. That is an engineering test, not a guarantee for every device, website or shutdown scenario.</p><h2>Know the boundaries</h2><p>Keep Chrome running. Browser shutdown and operating-system suspension remain uncertified. Recent unsubmitted edits use a short draft-save delay; do not assume a selection was submitted because you closed the popup. If Chrome asks where to save every file, review its download settings before a large batch.</p><p>Check errors on reopening. A failed attempt that is retried remains distinguishable from an unresolved failure. Retry only after investigating the cause; an access restriction requires permission from the source, not repeated attempts to bypass it.</p>''')]
(src/'posts.json').write_text(json.dumps(posts,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))
